-- Node: kd-initial-tx (3 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":3,"title":"Initial Treatment: IVIG and Aspirin","body":"**Treat as soon as the diagnosis is made**, ideally within 10 days of fever onset. After day 10, still treat if fever persists without another cause, or coronary arteries are abnormal, with ongoing inflammation. **Get the echo at diagnosis, but it never delays treatment.** [1][2][3]\n\n• [IVIG](#/drug/ivig/kawasaki initial) **2 g/kg IV once, over 8-12 hours.** Slower with shock or poor heart function. [2]\n• [Aspirin](#/drug/aspirin/kawasaki acute) **moderate 30-50 or high 80-100 mg/kg/day PO split every 6 hours**, until afebrile for 48-72 hours. Either is acceptable: the optimal dose is unclear and high dose has not been shown to protect the coronaries better. Then **3-5 mg/kg once daily** until the 4-6 week echo. [1][2][3]\n\n**Aspirin cautions:**\n• **No ibuprofen** or other NSAIDs: they block aspirin''s antiplatelet effect. [1]\n• **Influenza or recent varicella vaccine:** Reye syndrome risk. Discuss an alternative antiplatelet drug and use acetaminophen for fever. Give inactivated flu vaccine only. [1]\n\nNext: check for **high risk**, which decides whether steroids are added to this first treatment.","citation":[1,2,3],"next":"kd-risk","recommendation":"Kawasaki disease: IVIG 2 g/kg as a single infusion over 8-12 hours plus aspirin (moderate 30-50 or high 80-100 mg/kg/day until afebrile 48-72 hours, then 3-5 mg/kg/day), given as soon as the diagnosis is made and ideally within 10 days of fever onset; do not delay for the echocardiogram.","confidence":"recommended","calculatorLinks":[{"id":"kawasaki-doses","label":"Kawasaki Doses"}],"summary":"IVIG 2 g/kg over 8-12 h + aspirin 30-100 mg/kg/day; no ibuprofen","safetyLevel":"critical"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'kd-initial-tx' AND tree_id = 'kawasaki';

-- Node: kd-risk (4 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"question","module":4,"title":"High Risk at Diagnosis?","body":"**High risk for coronary aneurysms (AHA 2024):** [2]\n• Coronary **Z-score 2.5 or more** on the first echo\n• **Age under 6 months**\n• **High Son score** (3-5 points): 2 points for a baseline LAD or RCA Z-score of 2.0 or more; 1 each for age under 6 months, Asian race, CRP 13 mg/dL or more [4]\n\n**Shock or macrophage activation syndrome:** get an echo with coronary measurements (ACR strong recommendation), treat MAS with additional agents alongside IVIG, and involve PICU and rheumatology; these children often resist IVIG. [3][8]\n\nJapanese risk scores (Kobayashi, Egami, Sano) perform poorly in North American children. [3]","citation":[2,3,4,8],"options":[{"label":"Yes: high risk","description":"Add an adjunct (steroids, or infliximab) to the first IVIG","next":"kd-steroids","urgency":"urgent"},{"label":"No","description":"IVIG and aspirin alone","next":"kd-response"}],"calculatorLinks":[{"id":"kawasaki-high-risk","label":"High-Risk Screen"}],"summary":"Z 2.5+, age under 6 mo, Son 3+ = high risk; shock or MAS = specialist-directed therapy"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'kd-risk' AND tree_id = 'kawasaki';

-- Node: kd-steroids (4 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":4,"title":"High Risk: Add an Adjunct to the First IVIG","body":"**Adjuncts are added to IVIG and aspirin for high-risk patients, never instead of IVIG.** ACR 2021 conditionally recommends either:\n• **Glucocorticoids** (low certainty; the better-studied option), or\n• **A non-glucocorticoid immunomodulator** such as [infliximab](#/drug/infliximab/kawasaki), anakinra or cyclosporine (very low certainty). [3]\n\n**Steroids:**\n• [Prednisolone](#/drug/prednisolone/kawasaki) **2 mg/kg/day split every 8 hours (max 60 mg/day)**, IV first and then oral once afebrile; when CRP has normalized, **taper over 15 days** (RAISE regimen). In the US, where IV prednisolone is not marketed, the IV phase is given as methylprednisolone: agree the dose with pharmacy. [5]\n• **Evidence:** in RAISE (Japan; high risk defined by the Japanese Kobayashi score, not the North American criteria above), coronary abnormalities fell from 23% to 3%. [5]\n• **Strength:** ACR 2021 conditionally recommends IVIG plus glucocorticoids over IVIG alone for high-risk patients; AHA 2017 rates a longer steroid course with IVIG in high-risk patients **Class IIb**. [3][1]\n• **Not recommended:** a single pulse of methylprednisolone added to the first IVIG as routine therapy (AHA 2017 Class III). [1]\n\nChoose the adjunct with pediatric cardiology or rheumatology.","citation":[1,3,5],"next":"kd-response","recommendation":"High-risk Kawasaki disease (coronary Z-score 2.5 or more, age under 6 months, or high Son score): consider adding an adjunct to IVIG and aspirin with the specialist: a tapering corticosteroid course (prednisolone 2 mg/kg/day, max 60 mg/day, tapered over 15 days after CRP normalizes) or a non-glucocorticoid immunomodulator such as infliximab.","confidence":"consider","calculatorLinks":[{"id":"kawasaki-doses","label":"Kawasaki Doses"}],"summary":"Steroids (prednisolone 2 mg/kg/day, 15-day taper) or infliximab with the first IVIG"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'kd-steroids' AND tree_id = 'kawasaki';

-- Node: kd-resistant (1 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":5,"title":"IVIG-Resistant Kawasaki: Infliximab, Second IVIG or Steroids","body":"**Options** (with pediatric rheumatology or cardiology): [1][2][3][6]\n• **Second** [IVIG](#/drug/ivig/kawasaki resistant) **2 g/kg** over 8-12 hours. ACR 2021 conditionally prefers it over steroids; AHA 2017 Class IIa.\n• [Infliximab](#/drug/infliximab/kawasaki) **10 mg/kg IV once, over 2 hours** (KIDCARE dose; AHA 2024 table). In KIDCARE, infliximab settled fever more often than a second IVIG (77% vs 51% met the primary outcome), with shorter fever and hospital stay. AHA 2017 Class IIb.\n• **Steroids:** [pulse methylprednisolone](#/drug/methylprednisolone/kawasaki) 20-30 mg/kg IV daily for 1-3 days (max 1 g) with or without an oral taper, or a longer prednisolone course (AHA 2017 Class IIb).\n\n**Still not settling:** cyclosporine, anakinra, or other immunomodulators with specialist input (AHA 2017 Class IIb). [1][2]\n\n**Infliximab with the first IVIG:** in an unselected trial it did not reduce IVIG resistance (11% in both groups), though fever ended a day sooner. [7] ACR 2021 still allows it as a first-line adjunct for high-risk children (conditional, very low certainty): see [High Risk](#/node/kd-steroids). [3]","citation":[1,2,3,6,7],"next":"kd-coronary","recommendation":"IVIG-resistant Kawasaki disease (fever 36 hours or more after IVIG): give a second IVIG 2 g/kg or infliximab 10 mg/kg IV, or corticosteroids, with pediatric rheumatology or cardiology.","confidence":"recommended","calculatorLinks":[{"id":"kawasaki-doses","label":"Kawasaki Doses"}],"summary":"Second IVIG 2 g/kg or infliximab 10 mg/kg (or steroids); specialist input","safetyLevel":"warning"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'kd-resistant' AND tree_id = 'kawasaki';

-- Node: kd-dispo (1 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":8,"title":"Disposition and Follow-Up","body":"• **Admit all children** with Kawasaki disease being treated, or with incomplete Kawasaki under evaluation who need serial labs and echocardiography.\n• **PICU:** shock, myocardial dysfunction, macrophage activation syndrome, or giant aneurysms needing anticoagulation.\n• **Pediatric cardiology** for every patient; rheumatology for high-risk or IVIG-resistant disease.\n• **Discharge** once afebrile on low-dose aspirin, with echo follow-up at 1-2 weeks and 4-6 weeks. [1][2]\n• **Vaccines:** defer measles and varicella vaccines for **11 months** after IVIG (the IVIG blocks them). While on aspirin, give inactivated influenza vaccine, not the live nasal vaccine (Reye syndrome risk). [1][2]\n• **Tell families:** return for new fever, and avoid ibuprofen while on aspirin.","citation":[1,2],"recommendation":"Admit children treated for Kawasaki disease with pediatric cardiology involvement; arrange echocardiography at 1-2 and 4-6 weeks and defer measles and varicella vaccines for 11 months after IVIG.","confidence":"recommended","summary":"Admit; cardiology; echo at 1-2 and 4-6 weeks; defer MMR/varicella 11 months"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'kd-dispo' AND tree_id = 'kawasaki';

-- Node: vkdb-start (1 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":1,"title":"VKDB: Recognition","body":"[VKDB Steps Summary](#/info/vkdb-summary): stepwise approach and counseling.\n\n**Newborns are born low in vitamin K**, and breast milk contains little. Without the vitamin K shot at birth, the clotting factors that need it (II, VII, IX, X) can fall low enough to bleed.\n\n**Think VKDB** in any bleeding infant who **did not get the IM vitamin K shot** (declined, home or birthing-center birth), especially if **exclusively breastfed**, and in any infant with **cholestasis** (prolonged jaundice, pale stools), even after the shot. [1][3]\n\n**Warning bleeds often come first:** oozing from the umbilical stump, bruising, blood in stool or vomit, nosebleed, oozing after a heel stick, vaccination or circumcision. Take them seriously.\n\n**Without the shot, late VKDB affects about 4.4-7.2 per 100,000 infants** (the CDC estimated the risk at up to 81 times that with the shot), and 30-60% of late cases present as an intracranial hemorrhage. [2]","citation":[1,2,3],"next":"vkdb-ich-screen","images":[{"src":"images/vkdb/vkdb-timeline.svg","alt":"Timeline from birth to 6 months showing three types of vitamin K deficiency bleeding. Early, first 24 hours: maternal anticonvulsants, warfarin, rifampin or isoniazid; scalp, intracranial and intra-abdominal bleeding. Classic, days 2 to 7: no vitamin K at birth; gut, umbilical, circumcision and heel-stick bleeding. Late, 2 weeks to 6 months with a peak at 3 to 8 weeks: exclusively breastfed without IM vitamin K, or cholestasis; intracranial bleeding in 30 to 60 percent.","caption":"The three types of VKDB by age at onset. (myMedKitt original diagram, after Shearer 2009 and AAP 2022.)"}],"calculatorLinks":[{"id":"vkdb-type-risk","label":"VKDB Type and Risk"}],"summary":"No vitamin K shot + bleeding infant = VKDB until proven otherwise","safetyLevel":"warning"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'vkdb-start' AND tree_id = 'vkdb';

-- Node: vkdb-ich (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":2,"title":"Intracranial Hemorrhage","body":"**Treat before the scan result.** Draw labs first (PT/INR, PTT, CBC, fibrinogen, type and crossmatch, PIVKA-II if available), then give treatment without waiting for results:\n\n• [Vitamin K](#/drug/vitamin-k/vkdb treatment) **1 mg slow IV** (no faster than 1 mg/min): SC absorption is unreliable when perfusion is poor, and a brain bleed is the case where SC is not feasible. **Boxed warning:** severe and fatal anaphylactoid reactions can occur with IV (and IM) vitamin K, even when diluted and given slowly and on the first dose; the label reserves IV for when SC is not feasible. Give no faster than 1 mg/min and be ready to treat anaphylaxis.\n• **Plus factor replacement** for a brain bleed: [4-factor PCC](#/drug/pcc-4factor/vkdb) (off-label in infants; low-quality evidence, case series) or [fresh frozen plasma](#/drug/ffp/vkdb) 15-20 mL/kg. Choose with hematology and pharmacy. [4][5]\n• **Non-contrast head CT**, PICU and pediatric neurosurgery.\n• Treat seizures, protect the airway, keep normal temperature, glucose and blood pressure.\n\nRecheck the PT/INR after treatment. Use the [VKDB Doses](#/calculator/vkdb-doses) calculator for weight-based doses.","citation":[4,5],"next":"vkdb-abuse","recommendation":"Infant with suspected VKDB and intracranial hemorrhage: give vitamin K 1 mg slow IV (boxed warning: anaphylactoid reactions) plus factor replacement (4-factor PCC, off-label, or fresh frozen plasma) without waiting for coagulation results; obtain head CT and involve PICU and neurosurgery.","confidence":"recommended","calculatorLinks":[{"id":"vkdb-doses","label":"VKDB Doses"}],"summary":"Vitamin K IV + PCC (or FFP) now, head CT, PICU, neurosurgery","safetyLevel":"critical"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'vkdb-ich' AND tree_id = 'vkdb';

-- Node: vkdb-treat-major (3 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":4,"title":"Major Bleeding","body":"• [Vitamin K](#/drug/vitamin-k/vkdb treatment) **1 mg slow IV** (subcutaneous absorption is unreliable in shock). **Boxed warning:** severe and fatal anaphylactoid reactions can occur with IV (and IM) vitamin K, even when diluted and given slowly and on the first dose; the label reserves IV for when SC is not feasible. Give no faster than 1 mg/min and be ready to treat anaphylaxis.\n• **Factor replacement now:** [4-factor PCC](#/drug/pcc-4factor/vkdb), about 30 units/kg (20-50 units/kg reported in infant case series; off-label, low-quality evidence; dose with hematology and pharmacy), or [fresh frozen plasma](#/drug/ffp/vkdb) 15-20 mL/kg. Vitamin K takes hours to work; factors work in minutes. [4][5]\n• Packed red cells for significant blood loss.\n• Recheck PT/INR after factors and again at about 6-12 hours; repeat vitamin K if still prolonged. [3]","citation":[3,4,5],"next":"vkdb-abuse","recommendation":"Major VKDB bleeding: vitamin K 1 mg slow IV (boxed warning: anaphylactoid reactions) plus 4-factor PCC (off-label) or fresh frozen plasma 15-20 mL/kg; recheck PT/INR and repeat vitamin K if prolonged.","confidence":"recommended","calculatorLinks":[{"id":"vkdb-doses","label":"VKDB Doses"}],"summary":"Vitamin K IV + PCC or FFP; recheck INR","safetyLevel":"critical"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'vkdb-treat-major' AND tree_id = 'vkdb';

-- Node: vkdb-treat-minor (3 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":4,"title":"Minor Bleeding","body":"• [Vitamin K](#/drug/vitamin-k/vkdb treatment) **1 mg subcutaneously** (the label''s preferred route; the label also allows IM for treating newborn bleeding). Use slow IV only if SC is not feasible: the boxed warning reports severe and fatal reactions with IV and IM use. [5]\n• Local pressure for oozing sites.\n• Recheck PT/INR in about 6-12 hours: it should be improving. Repeat vitamin K if it remains prolonged. [3]\n• A baby with a \"minor\" bleed and no vitamin K can still have a brain bleed: low threshold for head imaging if there is any neurologic change, vomiting or irritability.","citation":[3,5],"next":"vkdb-abuse","recommendation":"Minor VKDB bleeding: vitamin K 1 mg subcutaneously (IM allowed by the label; IV only if SC is not feasible), local measures, and recheck PT/INR within hours.","confidence":"recommended","summary":"Vitamin K 1 mg SC; recheck INR; low threshold for CT"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'vkdb-treat-minor' AND tree_id = 'vkdb';

-- Node: vkdb-abuse (1 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":5,"title":"Inflicted Injury and Other Causes","body":"**VKDB and abuse can look alike** (subdural hemorrhage, bruising, retinal bleeding in severe cases), and **one does not exclude the other**. [7]\n\n• **Normal coagulation screen + intracranial bleed or bruising** in a non-mobile infant: full inflicted-injury evaluation (skeletal survey, ophthalmology, child protection team) per your protocol.\n• **Prolonged PT that corrects with vitamin K**, no vitamin K at birth, and injuries limited to bleeding: supports VKDB, but the child protection team still decides when injuries are unexplained. [7]\n\n**Other causes of a bleeding infant:**\n• **Isolated long PTT:** hemophilia A or B.\n• **Low platelets:** neonatal alloimmune thrombocytopenia, maternal ITP, infection.\n• **Low platelets with a long PT or low fibrinogen:** DIC (sepsis).\n• **Long PT that does not correct:** liver failure.","citation":[7],"next":"vkdb-cause","calculatorLinks":[{"id":"vkdb-lab-pattern","label":"Lab Pattern"}],"summary":"VKDB does not exclude abuse; normal coags + ICH = abuse workup","safetyLevel":"warning"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'vkdb-abuse' AND tree_id = 'vkdb';

-- Node: vkdb-prevention (2 field(s) changed)
UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"info","module":7,"title":"Prevention and Parents Who Decline","body":"**Standard prophylaxis (AAP):** a single [vitamin K](#/drug/vitamin-k/vkdb prophylaxis) dose **1 mg IM** soon after birth (within 6 hours per AAP; the label says within 1 hour) for infants over 1500 g; **0.3-0.5 mg/kg IM** for 1500 g or less. [1]\n\n**Oral vitamin K is less protective** against late VKDB, and there is no FDA-approved oral neonatal product in the US. Oral regimens used in Europe need repeated doses for weeks and fail more often, especially with cholestasis. [1][3]\n\n**Counseling families who decline:**\n• Without the shot, late VKDB affects about 4.4-7.2 per 100,000 babies (CDC estimate: up to 81 times the risk with the shot), and it often presents as a brain bleed. [2]\n• The single IM dose is safe; the old concern about childhood cancer was not confirmed. [1]\n• Most parents in the Tennessee cluster had not known the risk before their baby bled. [2]\n• Make sure the next sibling gets the shot.","citation":[1,2,3],"next":"vkdb-dispo","summary":"1 mg IM at birth; oral is less protective; counsel declining families"}'::jsonb
WHERE id = 'vkdb-prevention' AND tree_id = 'vkdb';

COMMIT;